deeper.body:
The page says the system 'finds statistical structure in these examples that eventually allows the system to come up with rules'. Beyond the book: the word *statistical* does real work. A symbolic rule is exact ('a bishop moves diagonally'); a learned rule is a tendency ('images with this texture distribution are usually labelled food'). Consequences you will meet throughout the book:

- learned rules can be wrong on individual examples even when they are right on average;
- they depend on the examples seen — a system trained on one photographer's pictures may fail on another's (generalization, Chapter 5);
- they are only as good as the labels — mislabelled pictures teach wrong tendencies.

This is also why the authors insist the field is empirical: the only way to know whether the statistical rules are good is to measure them on data.
